.TH LDDCP 1 "2026-08-12" "lddcp 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lddcp \- copy required shared libraries into a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lddcp
.RI "[\-d "
.IR directory ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RI "[\-l "
.IR library ]...
.RI "[\-p "
.IR program ]...
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lddcp
recursively searches for the shared libraries required by one or more programs
(or libraries) and copies them into a destination folder, preserving the full
absolute path of each library relative to the root directory.
It is the equivalent of running
.BR ldd "(1)"
and then manually copying everything it reports.
.PP
For each input,
.B lddcp
parses the ELF headers to read the
.B PT_INTERP
segment (the dynamic linker) and the
.B DT_NEEDED
entries (the shared libraries it depends on).
Each
.B DT_NEEDED
name is searched for in the directories parsed from
.I /etc/ld.so.conf
and its included files, plus a set of standard multiarch fallback paths.
.PP
If the resolved path of a needed library is a symbolic link,
.B lddcp
records every intermediate link and the final real file.
Real files are copied byte-for-byte and symlinks are recreated as symlinks
pointing to the same target, so the destination reflects the on-disk layout of
the source.
.PP
Every newly discovered library is itself scanned for its
.B DT_NEEDED
entries, which are also processed.
.B lddcp
tracks visited paths to avoid rescans, redundant copies, and dependency loops.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " directory"
Destination directory where the shared libraries will be copied to.
Required.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a help message and exit.
.TP
.BI \-l " library"
Library to scan for shared libraries.
The library itself will also be copied to the destination folder.
May be given several times.
At least one of
.B \-p
or
.B \-l
is required.
.TP
.BI \-p " program"
Program to scan for shared libraries.
The program itself is not copied, only its dependencies are.
May be given several times.
At least one of
.B \-p
or
.B \-l
is required.
.TP
.B \-v
Show the program's version number and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Copy the dependencies of
.I /bin/sh
into
.I /tmp/requirements
(including the dynamic linker):
.IP
.EX
$ lddcp -p /bin/sh -d /tmp/requirements
.EE
.PP
Copy the dependencies of several programs using several
.B \-p
options:
.IP
.EX
$ lddcp -p /bin/sh -p /bin/ls -p /bin/ln -d /tmp/requirements
.EE
.PP
Copy a specific library and its dependencies, for example to bundle a
dependency that a program searches for at run time:
.IP
.EX
$ lddcp -l libnss_dns.so.2 -d /tmp/requirements
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
Also returned by
.B \-h
and
.B \-v\fR.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid option or a missing option argument.
.TP
.B 2
Missing required option (\-d or at least one of \-p/\-l).
.TP
.B 3
The destination directory is invalid or could not be created.
.SH NOTES
.B ldd
reports
.I linux-vdso.so.1
(the virtual dynamic shared object injected by the kernel), which has no file on
disk.
.B lddcp
silently skips any needed library it cannot find on disk.
.PP
Libraries are looked up only by name in the configured search paths. If a
needed library cannot be found, a warning is printed to standard error and the
scan continues.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ld.so.conf
Configuration file listing library search paths. Included files are read
recursively and existing directories only are retained.
.SH AUTHOR
Frederico Martins
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ldd (1),
.BR ldconfig (8),
.BR ld.so (8)
